	"notes": "This is a documentation site built the way most docs sites are: a link tree on the left, the article in the middle, and a short table of contents on the right. What makes it worth a second look is HOW the three columns share the room — Wikipedia and MDN give their side rails a fixed number of pixels that never changes, like a ruler taped to the page, but Kubernetes gives all three columns a PERCENTAGE of the window instead. That sounds like a small difference, but it means the link tree and the table of contents keep growing on a wider screen even though a list of short links does not get more useful at 570px wide, and the middle reading column can stretch past 2200px with no cap at all — probably wider than is comfortable to read. Below 768px, the whole thing collapses to one column: the link tree becomes a closed accordion you have to tap open, and the table of contents disappears.",
